<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ephorus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400–<a href="/years/330-bce">330 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ephorus of Cyme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ephorus of Cyme was a Greek historian of the 4th century BCE, said to have been a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a>. His Histories in 30 books were the first universal history, arranged by subject from the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracleidae">Heracleidae</a> to 340 BCE. The work is lost but was used heavily by later writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ephorus was born in Cyme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolia</a> and, with the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theopompus">Theopompus</a>, was a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a> in rhetoric. Little else is known of his life. He does not seem to have made much progress as a speaker, and at the suggestion of Isocrates himself he took up literary composition and the study of history. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, he declined an offer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> to join the Persian campaign as official historian. His son Demophilus also became a historian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His main work was a universal history in 29 books. Demophilus edited the whole and added a thirtieth. It began with the days of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracleidae">Heraclids</a> and came down to the taking of Perinthus by Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in 340 BCE, covering more than seven hundred years, and it included a summary description of the Sacred Wars. Polybius says that Ephorus was the first historian to write a universal history. Each book had its own prooimion, and the work, probably called simply Historiai, followed a thematic rather than a strictly chronological order.</p><p>Besides the universal history he wrote the Epichorios logos, a patriotic essay praising the traditions of Cyme, a book on inventions, Peri heurematon, and a work on style, Peri lexeos. Other titles are attributed to him, though it is doubtful whether they were separate works or only extracts from the Histories. Of all these only isolated fragments survive. He also reported that a comet split in two in the winter of 372–373 BCE, a claim that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-the-younger">Seneca the Younger</a>, the ancient source for the report, rejected because nobody else had recorded it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The work was highly praised and widely read in antiquity, and later historians drew on it freely. It is generally believed to be the main or sole source for the account by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> of Greek history between 480 and 340 BCE, one of only two continuous narratives of the period that survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> valued Ephorus's geographical investigations and quoted him at length in his Geographica, praising him as the first to separate the historical from the geographical element. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> credited him with knowledge of naval warfare but ridiculed his account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-362-bce">Battle of Mantinea</a> in 362 BCE as showing ignorance of land operations.</p><p>Ancient writers regarded him as able and thorough but somewhat dull. He drew a sharp line, though not always, between myth and history, and he saw that a profusion of detail is grounds for suspicion in reports of distant history. His style was high-flown and artificial, and he often sacrificed truth to rhetorical effect. Dionysius of Halicarnassus judged that he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theopompus">Theopompus</a> were the only historians whose language was accurate and complete.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ephorus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ephorus</b> and Aristotle compared them with those of Sparta, and Plato set his last dialogue, the Laws, among three old men walking from Knossos to the cave of Zeus on Mount Ida, one of them a Knossian named Kleinias who is charged with founding a new Cretan city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 4th century BCE the historian <b>Ephorus</b> described &quot;a very prosperous market called Tartessos, with much tin carried by river, as well as gold and copper from Celtic lands&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tartessos">Tartessos · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The city joined the Ionian Revolt and later the Delian League, and the historian <b>Ephorus</b> was born there in the 4th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/cyme-aeolis">Cyme (Aeolis)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cyme, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.76,26.94&amp;year=-355" aria-label="Cyme on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5218 773 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ephorus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5668 1043h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5668 1043h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.76,26.94&amp;year=-355">What was here in 355 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
